<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Naxos (Sicily)</h1><p class="st-meta">734 BCE – 403 BCE (approx.) · capital Naxos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Greek colony in Sicily, founded by Chalcidians in 734 BCE beneath Mount Etna and destroyed by Dionysius I in 403 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, Chalcidians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocles">Theocles</a> founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a> in 734 BCE on the Schisò headland below modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a>; later writers add settlers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> island of Naxos, which gave the city its name. As the first landfall for ships crossing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, it became the starting point for Greek colonisation of the island.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a> was small but symbolically important: its altar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> Archegetes, 'the Founder', was where envoys sailing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> made sacrifice. Within a few years it sent out settlers to found <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/leontini">Leontini</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/catana">Catana</a>. It grew vines on the slopes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-etna">Etna</a>, and its coins with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> and a satyr are celebrated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates-of-gela">Hippocrates of Gela</a> subdued it around 495 BCE, and later it was often allied with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> and Leontini against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I</a> captured <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a> by treachery in 403 BCE, sold its people into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and gave its land to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicels">Sicels</a>. In 358 BCE survivors and Sicels founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tauromenium">Tauromenium</a> on the hill above. Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giardini-naxos">Giardini Naxos</a> have revealed the walls, sanctuaries and street grid.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/naxos-sicily.jpg" alt="Naxos (Sicily)" width="320" height="244"><figcaption>Image: Alun Salt from Derby and Leicester, UK · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walls_at_Naxos,_Sicily_(71735239).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>734 BCE – 403 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Naxos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sicilian Naxos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-568&amp;focus=naxos-sicily" aria-label="Naxos (Sicily) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5061 818 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Naxos (Sicily) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5381 1074L5383 1099M5381 1074L5381 1073"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5383 1099h0M5381 1073h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 1074h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 1074h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-568&amp;focus=naxos-sicily">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
